thinking_steps:
The question asks if the sharpness is more important than the speed.
The comment section provides a lot of insight into why the blades are moving slow.
@1pacarr: Very simply explained for those that seem confused, if the camera shoots at 60 Frames per second and the blade spins at 60 revolutions per second the blade will look stationary as the revolutions and frames are synced.  In this video, the blade is moving around 70 revolutions per second, so  the blade looks like it's moving extremely slowly.
@Beast_ASP_Official: For those who didn't understand this : this is basically that cutter moving with 170rpm but the camera recorded it in 50fps, camera's 1 frame captures 3.4 rounds that's why it seems to look slow but it's fast enough to cut the cucumber in 80 pieces in 1 second.

This shows that the blades are not moving slowly, but the camera is recording them at a frequency that creates the illusion of slow movement. Therefore, the blades are cutting so quickly because of their speed, and the blade sharpness is also a factor.
